, 16, 21, 16, 2372, 92857, 16382, 513, 3354, 539, 9662, 11, 864, 7346, 430, 264, 1067, 314, 279, 6797, 579, 58302, 22842, 314, 279, 8964, 13, 3095, 9662, 43695, 888, 279, 17415, 11, 424, 11121, 264, 3878, 314, 16382, 13, 1079, 34, 8778, 1730, 93291, 10580, 2372, 9205, 16382, 513, 524, 1066, 279, 12961, 9155, 3294, 279, 2919, 11, 694, 12663, 1441, 310, 279, 17415, 6220, 13, 1577, 16382, 5640, 8574, 279, 29199, 11, 279, 5445, 919, 314, 279, 11717, 1305, 1964, 3055, 279, 17415, 6220, 1345, 279, 8156, 919, 10480, 10281, 13, 1577, 279, 17415, 7739, 40664, 11784, 310, 29199, 11, 279, 10602, 383, 279, 11717, 579, 5445, 8772, 15766, 321, 279, 8156, 919, 11690, 310, 35003, 4487, 13, 271, 55025, 198, 32, 25, 5435, 4748, 17675, 1892, 561, 1965, 5435, 39072, 2144, 13, 198, 33, 25, 7021, 1892, 561, 2400, 1965, 369, 7021, 539, 279, 16713, 2450, 13, 271, 5423, 1132, 279, 2904, 2306, 13, 198, 15666, 25], "raw_logits_by_display_label": {" A": 14.905085563659668, " B": 16.458541870117188}, "raw_logits_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 14.905085563659668, "supported": 16.458541870117188}, "readout_s": 3.0416999834415037e-05, "request_id": "sha256:26e98a0289c87403dcde0ad9", "requested_score_position": 469, "response_sha256": "sha256:1c18fe1facc5694c61b688cf17bd4b20893db290080346a8667796ade1622aaa", "response_text": "Ocean waves are created by wind, which forms as a result of the sun's uneven heating of the Earth. When wind blows over the ocean, it creates a series of waves. These waves are not just the portion visible above the water, but extend down to the ocean floor. As waves travel toward the shore, the bottom part of the wave drags along the ocean floor while the upper part moves faster. As the ocean depth decreases closer to shore, the drag on the wave's bottom becomes stronger and the upper part begins to tilt forward.", "role": "training", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:5ff73a56114aa2e199535703623f7ed0c23b525481eb8999e586ff3c1a6c7fe5", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 470, "sentence_count": 2, "sentence_version": "carnot-utf8-sentence-v1", "source_group_id": "group-4354acfd396589c7eceb468a", "source_sha256": "sha256:2ac56b6433646972f590123cfae03b2ff06b0fd81999b91699bf100aa1bc8a22", "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:Wave Energy Fundamentals: How Waves Form. Wave energy, in one sense, is just another form of solar energy. This might sound odd, but just consider that waves start from wind, which forms as a result of the sun's heating of the \u00adEarth. The sun doesn't ever heat the Earth evenly.\\n\\npassage 2:To get a sense of wind-created waves, simply blow over a bowl of liquid, for instance. A series of waves will follow your exhalation's path. Even when wind is blowing out to sea, waves still come ashore as a result of the shape of the ocean basins.\\n\\npassage 3:The wave visible above the level of the water is in fact only part of the wave; the wave extends down through the water column to the ocean floor as well. Waves start out in the deep, open ocean as relatively vertical in shape, Presnell said. As a wave travels toward the shore, though, the bottom part of the wave drags along the ocean floor. The upper part of the wave above the water line starts to move faster than the rest of the wave. As the ocean depth decreases closer to shore, the drag on the wave's bottom becomes stronger, and the upper part of the wave begins to tilt forward.\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"how ocean waves work\"}", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.0009757239995451528, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id": "248046", "metadata_model": "gpt2", "representation": "embedded_gguf"}, "window_index": 0, "window_sha256": "sha256:a413a9afa4877c6ab3477ba0cec7eb3c7b09111c1a70726e18e8908bfffdbddc", "window_version": "carnot-balanced-contiguous-v1"}, {"actual_