7ff13058125665a5d041fab425cff8aab7a732afb0b2c19180f0456c69c", "response_text": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between HALT and HASS are:\n\n1. Purpose: HALT is used for stress testing during the engineering development phase of a product to reveal design weaknesses, while HASS is used as a production screen to identify weak components and ensure they are properly tested.\n2. Focus: HALT focuses on finding design weaknesses through accelerated stress testing, while HASS focuses on identifying weak components and evaluating their performance under different stresses.\n3. Stress levels: HALT uses higher stress levels than HASS to expose potential design weaknesses early in the development process.\n4. Environment: HALT is conducted in a controlled environment, while HASS is conducted in a production environment.\n\nTherefore, the main differences between HALT and HASS are:\n\nHALT (Highly Accelerated Life Testing):\n\n* Used during engineering development phase\n* Focuses on finding design weaknesses through accelerated stress testing\n* Uses higher stress levels than HASS\n* Conducted in a controlled environment\n\nHASS (Highly Accelerated Screening System):\n\n* Used as a production screen\n* Focuses on identifying weak components and evaluating their performance under different stresses\n* Uses lower stress levels than HALT\n* Conducted in a production environment", "role": "training", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:667307808188f76501b99361db419229cad094cc3bf4479731ab0301d9a8d484", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 535, "sentence_count": 1, "sentence_version": "carnot-utf8-sentence-v1", "source_group_id": "group-5600b09990bd16d558d42a35", "source_sha256": "sha256:21beaac48694fe34155c705e43147e97b82c486c8867118f87b8b37f935d7e9c", "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:HALT, HASS and ESS. Highly Accelerated Life Testing (HALT) Highly Accelerated Life Testing (HALT) is a stress testing methodology used during the engineering development phase of a product to quickly reveal design weaknesses.\\n\\npassage 2:HALT is run during the design phase of a program to find design weaknesses and eliminate them. Any stress that exposes design weaknesses that would show up in the normal environments is suitable even if these stresses do not occur in the normal environments to which the product will be exposed.\\n\\npassage 3:The HASS process is a production screen to assure that weak components are precipitated (failed). HALT experience and the step stress empirical evaluation is used to establish a good screen. It is important that the level of stress is tailored to the product. There is no one-stress-fits-all.\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"what is the difference between halt and hass\"}", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.001767984000252909, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id": "248046", "metadata_model": "gpt2", "representation": "embedded_gguf"}, "window_index": 2, "window_sha256": "sha256:e60e2e1c1d007cc76906597670a23d69da7e05889f8dd22bf82618c0f6dfa362", "window_version": "carnot-balanced-contiguous-v1"}, {"actual_last_evaluated_position": 409, "archived_pilot_reuse": false, "arm": "whole_response", "attempted": true, "byte_end": null, "byte_start": null, "call_id": "sha256:9a732f222a06f8ff2f9d3f53", "disposition": "complete", "eligible": true, "error": null, "generated_tokens": 0, "gold_label": null, "group_id": "group-5627ee111ad139d98c730e5b", "kv_state_id": "reset-epoch-493", "label_to_option_id": {" A": "supported", " B": "contains_unsupported"}, "log_odds_contains_unsupported": 2.6763534545898438, "marked_response": null, "model_sha256": "sha256:7e78da5d7e3ae28d178121f58646953305f3e5bd3cb46f4a75584e8b6c6fe169", "native_option_token_ids": {" A": 357, " B": 417}, "normalization": "two_option_softmax_float64", "optio