5, 39072, 2144, 13, 271, 5423, 1132, 279, 2904, 2306, 13, 198, 15666, 25], "raw_logits_by_display_label": {" A": 15.20017147064209, " B": 16.05223846435547}, "raw_logits_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 16.05223846435547, "supported": 15.20017147064209}, "readout_s": 3.514499985612929e-05, "request_id": "sha256:b128480b0c13574411fc225b", "requested_score_position": 791, "response_sha256": "sha256:4ca57ef0720a33326f77f94e9ffe37c545f3164e6aa04b937482bfb105c73fe4", "response_text": "Based on the given passages, here's how to figure out the number of joists needed:\n\nPassage 1 provides a formula for calculating the square footage of lumber needed to sheath the floor. However, it does not provide any information on how to calculate the number of joists needed.\n\nPassage 2 and Passage 3 both suggest dividing the length of the room by 16 to determine the number of joists needed. This is because floor joists are typically placed no more than 16 inches apart.\n\nTherefore, to calculate the number of joists needed, you would need to know the length of the room in inches and then divide that number by 16.\n\nFor example, if the length of the room is 120 inches (10 feet), you would divide 120 by 16 to get 7.5. This means you would need 8 joists (since you can't have a fraction of a joist).\n\nSo, to summarize, the number of joists needed can be calculated by dividing the length of the room by 16.", "role": "training", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:57d998e09531fa743cb544dcf9ecdb23454b6d2e33596a88ca499a90bbc5d1a5", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 792, "sentence_count": null, "sentence_version": null, "source_group_id": "group-bb0d8a341e8f80874056baca", "source_sha256": "sha256:e56d6f2b10e778302dc39c34a90cf86214fe4fc449535d94b91225aa3c1a6750", "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:To figure out how much lumber is needed to sheath the floor just simply multiply the width by the length (equals square footage), add 10% for wastage and that\u2019s the square feet of lumber needed. If using plywood square footage divided by 30. Example 30 ft X 40 ft = 1200 SF divided by 30 = 40 sheets.o figure out how much lumber is needed to sheath the floor just simply multiply the width by the length (equals square footage), add 10% for wastage and that\u2019s the square feet of lumber needed. If using plywood square footage divided by 30. Example 30 ft X 40 ft = 1200 SF divided by 30 = 40 sheets.\\n\\npassage 2:Calculate the number of joists you need by dividing the length of the room by 16. You use 16 because the floor joists will be placed no more than 16 inches apart.Measure the distance between each floor joist. While the standard distance is 16 inches, the gaps may be smaller.Add these measurements together to determine the amount of lumber you need for the blocking.ou use 16 because the floor joists will be placed no more than 16 inches apart. Measure the distance between each floor joist. While the standard distance is 16 inches, the gaps may be smaller. Add these measurements together to determine the amount of lumber you need for the blocking.\\n\\npassage 3:Instructions. Measure the length and width of the room in inches. Calculate the number of joists you need by dividing the length of the room by 16. You use 16 because the floor joists will be placed no more than 16 inches apart. Measure the distance between each floor joist.While the standard distance is 16 inches, the gaps may be smaller.Add these measurements together to determine the amount of lumber you need for the blocking.ou use 16 because the floor joists will be placed no more than 16 inches apart. Measure the distance between each floor joist. While the standard distance is 16 inches, the gaps may be smaller. Add these measurements together to determine the amount of lumber you need for the blocking.\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"figure out number of joists needed\"}", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b