20 for 1000 square feet. Therefore, based on these estimates, drywall may be the more cost-effective option.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that these estimates do not include the cost of installation, which can add to the overall cost of either option. Additionally, the cost of materials and labor can vary depending on location and other factors, so it's important to get quotes from multiple contractors before making a decision.", "role": "training", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:c9d55d27f5ca9b64755176cda3ef78cddf8dd06e722086c99f553a601841ea4b", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 700, "sentence_count": null, "sentence_version": null, "source_group_id": "group-c0a47dc76ee63e411a5a9785", "source_sha256": "sha256:c8f77e1d2c010b3f5e1b0f74562e884f0cc9c3186ed7254772a403c616d00f37", "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:Basement Ceiling and Dropped Ceiling Tile Disadvantages. When deciding between dropped ceiling tiles vs drywall ceilings for your basement ceiling, it is important that you consider the height of your unfinished basement.The one key downside of installing dropped ceiling tiles is that your basement ceiling will be lower, compared to installing a drywall ceiling.You will need 3-4 inches of space between your main floor floor-joists and your dropped ceiling tiles.asement Ceiling and Dropped Ceiling Tile Disadvantages. When deciding between dropped ceiling tiles vs drywall ceilings for your basement ceiling, it is important that you consider the height of your unfinished basement.\\n\\npassage 2:Andrew says. Jason, Take a look a Ceiling Link, it\u2019s a PVC based \u201cdrop ceiling\u201d system that only costs you about 1\u2033 of space, only twice the space of drywall. A 1000 SQ Ft basement (assuming 20X50) would run you $707 in ceiling link parts, $40 in screws and $700 to 900 in panels (2\u2032 X 4\u2032).rywall for the basement ceiling, .5 inch. Carpet and padding maybe .75 of an inch. Otherwise there are no framing elements on the ceiling or the floor. Unless you want install a framed sub-floor, but that\u2019s not the normal case for most finished basements.\\n\\npassage 3:The cost to install drywall is about $1.50 per square foot. After material and labor are added in, the cost per panel can range from around $40.00 to $60.00. A typical 12x12 room, for example, will use 12 panels.he cost to install drywall is about $1.50 per square foot. After material and labor are added in, the cost per panel can range from around $40.00 to $60.00. A typical 12x12 room, for example, will use 12 panels.\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"drywall vs ceiling tile cost\"}", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.0012689229997704388, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id": "248046", "metadata_model": "gpt2", "representation": "embedded_gguf"}, "window_index": null, "window_sha256": null, "window_version": null}, {"actual_last_evaluated_position": 719, "archived_pilot_reuse": false, "arm": "focused_window", "attempted": true, "byte_end": 236, "byte_start": 0, "call_id": "sha256:d96661f728e5818c13b75ff1", "disposition": "complete", "eligible": true, "error": null, "generated_tokens": 0, "gold_label": null, "group_id": "group-c0a47dc76ee63e411a5a9785", "kv_state_id": "reset-epoch-1058", "label_to_option_id": {" A": "supported", " B": "contains_unsupported"}, "log_odds_contains_unsupported": 2.699735641479492, "marked_response": "<<CARNOT_FOCUS_START:0:236>>Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nThe cost of drywall versus ceiling tiles for a basement ceiling can vary depending on several factors, including the size of the room and the specific materials used. <<CARNOT_FOCUS_END>>However, according to Passage 2, the cost of ceiling tiles can be approximately $707 for 1000 square feet, while the cost of drywall can ra