": 14.935385704040527, " B": 13.242300033569336}, "raw_logits_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 14.935385704040527, "supported": 13.242300033569336}, "readout_s": 3.806200038525276e-05, "request_id": "sha256:0d04f5c7273c7716a723f497", "requested_score_position": 763, "response_sha256": "sha256:34352fa101b9aa958b10b94e58fd658d6eea333b56f67abacba81000a19302ec", "response_text": "Sure! Here's the summary in 109 words:\n\nISIS fighters seized several districts in Ramadi, Iraq after an hour-long attack that left 10 Iraqi security forces dead. The attack marked a setback for Iraqi forces, who have been trying to regain control of the city for months. The group used suicide and car bombs, and the head of the Iraqi military operation in Anbar province was wounded. The captured districts are located just 70 miles west of Baghdad. Despite recent victories against ISIS, the group still poses a threat and remains capable of launching offensive operations.", "role": "calibration_tuning", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:2a31ca24ce7a163820430289bb4041cb1280f4d0d11ffa17b21566ddb6078ff9", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 764, "sentence_count": 2, "sentence_version": "carnot-utf8-sentence-v1", "source_group_id": "group-48daf3a901cf61f0c6e810a4", "source_sha256": "sha256:0b3e14b7509fc4aa6ba117ee9d478270ce06042df884c07e30647e97807c85ef", "source_text": "ISIS fighters seized several districts in the Iraqi city of Ramadi in an hours-long assault Friday that included suicide and car bombs, an Iraqi provincial official said. At least 10 Iraqi security forces were killed in the attacks, according to Faleh al-Essawi, the deputy head of Iraq's Anbar provincial council. And the head of the Iraqi military operation in Anbar province, Gen. Qassim al-Muhammadi, was wounded. The northern Ramadi districts of Albu Faraj, Albu Essa and Albu Risha were in the hands of ISIS by the time the day was done. Located about 70 miles (110 kilometers) west of Baghdad, Ramadi has seen intense and persistent fighting for months. ISIS took over parts of that western Anbar city in the first half of 2014, and it's been part of a tug of war ever since. Iraqi security forces discovered tunnels in February that they say could have led opposition fighters to a central government compound in the city.  But they didn't find all of them: A few weeks later, ISIS detonated hundreds of homemade bombs from a tunnel underneath an army headquarters there, according to Sabah Al-Karhout, the head of the Anbar provincial council. More than 40 Iraqi soldiers died in that explosion. Iraqi and allied forces have made inroads in recent weeks, beating back the group that calls itself the Islamic State, which took over vast swaths of Iraq and neighboring Syria last year. Their most high-profile victory, for instance, was the recapture of Tikrit. And U.S.-led airstrikes have already made a difference, according to officials in Washington and beyond. Still, ISIS remains a formidable force and, as the Ramadi assault shows, one that's still capable and willing to go on offense to take territory. That may be best illustrated by the case of Mosul, Iraq's second largest city and the site of one of its biggest embarrassments when Iraqi soldiers and police dropped their weapons and fled their posts as ISIS forces advanced last June. A U.S. official said in February that up to 25,000 Iraqis troops were expected to return to the key northern Iraqi city in April or May. But, on Thursday, a senior official in U.S. President Barack Obama's administration appeared to back away from that timeline -- saying an Iraqi-led assault on Mosul \"might be some time from now (or) might be soon.\" Calling for \"patience,\" an administration official said that winning Mosul is a complex endeavor. The same could be said for the entire effort to defeat ISIS. It will \"take a lot of capacity,\" the official said, \"and some time to build.\" CNN's Kim Acosta contributed to this report.\n", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.001648952999858011, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id": "248046", "metadata_model": "gpt2", "representation": "embedded_gguf"}, "window_index": 0, "window_sha256": "sha256:3970935a34149ea6342e2c5783377c377ff6d17402f016681d6671a5b17b564d", "window_version": "carnot-balanced-contiguous-v1"}, {"actual_last_evaluated_position": 765, "archived_pilot_reuse": false, "arm": "focused_window", "attempted": true, "byte_end": 385, "byte_start": 162, "call_id": "sha256:c5a06c87d0a798a50305a7da", "disposition": "complete", "eligible": true, "error": null, "generated_tokens": 0, "gold_label": null, "group_id": "group-48daf3a901cf61f0c6e810a4", "kv_state_id": "reset-epoch-1525", "label_to_option_id": {" A": "contains_unsupported", " B": "supported"}, "log_odds_contains_unsupported": 1.6368207931518555, "marked_response": "Sure! Here's the summary in 109 words:\n\nISIS fighters seized several districts in Ramadi, Iraq after an hour-long attack that left 10 Iraqi security forces dead. <<CARNOT_FOCUS_START:162:385>>The attack marked a setback for Iraqi forces, who have been trying to regain control of the city for months. The group used suicide and car bombs, and the head of the Iraqi military operation in Anbar province was wounded. <<CARNOT_FOCUS_END>>The captured districts are located just 70 miles west of Baghdad. Despite recent victories against ISIS, the group still poses a threat and remains capable of launching offensive operations.", "model_sha256": "sha256:7e78da5d7e3ae28d178121f58646953305f3e5bd3cb46f4a75584e8b6c6fe169", "native_option_token_ids": {" A": 357, " B": 417}, "normalization": "two_option_softmax_float64", "option_order": ["contains_unsupported", "supported"], "order_remapping": {" A": "contains_unsupported", " B": "supported"}, "prefill_s": 0.9050633729993933, 