, 5601, 1472, 427, 8714, 321, 23522, 11036, 13, 271, 55025, 198, 32, 25, 7021, 1892, 561, 2400, 1965, 369, 7021, 539, 279, 16713, 2450, 13, 198, 33, 25, 5435, 4748, 17675, 1892, 561, 1965, 5435, 39072, 2144, 13, 271, 5423, 1132, 279, 2904, 2306, 13, 198, 15666, 25], "raw_logits_by_display_label": {" A": 15.66370964050293, " B": 15.93449878692627}, "raw_logits_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 15.93449878692627, "supported": 15.66370964050293}, "readout_s": 3.3692999750201125e-05, "request_id": "sha256:1b49c6a8e7425bd552afe751", "requested_score_position": 671, "response_sha256": "sha256:3e118d359eecd93f22427f440c2f6bc306bf4ef3b4d6ea241f937637bf5017a2", "response_text": "The brewing process of beer consists of eight key steps: malting, milling, mashing, brewing, cooling, fermentation, racking and finishing. In the malting process, the grain is added to a vat along with water and allowed to soak for about 40 hours. After that, the mash is created and then separated from the spent grain to extract the sugary liquid known as wort, this step is called lautering. The wort is then put into a large brew kettle for the boiling process, where hops are added at very specific times to ensure consistency in taste. After boiling, the mixture is cooled. For an ale, ale yeast is added and the temperature is allowed to rise to between 65-76 degrees, creating a thick layer of foam at the top of the fermentation vessel. The beer then goes through racking and finishing processes.", "role": "calibration_tuning", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:460b03f48888462d3f53d1efec052ce6d706beb17d710a783b16d06af87d7fef", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 672, "sentence_count": 2, "sentence_version": "carnot-utf8-sentence-v1", "source_group_id": "group-591e609f1d5d6de4cf4782d5", "source_sha256": "sha256:6aab2236b09213f3b1dc04d62f19996f03822bf76e5aae8dad7d68d785063786", "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:If brewing an ale, which account for the majority of beers by style, the brewer will pitch ale yeast and let the temperature of the fermenting beer rise to a toasty 65-76 degrees \u2013 perfect for ale yeast.Also called top-fermenting yeast, ale yeast forms a thick layer pillowy foam at the top of the fermentation vessel.autering. The next step in the brewing process is the take the mash, and separate out the spent grain from the sugary liquid known as wort (pronounced wert). This process is called lautering. To begin the lautering process, the mash is transfered to vessel with a false-bottom called a lauter tun.\\n\\npassage 2:The Brewing Process. The brewing process consists of eight key components: malting, milling, mashing, brewing, cooling, fermentation, racking and finishing. Malting: The grain is added to a vat along with water and allowed to soak for about 40 hours.he Brewing Process. The brewing process consists of eight key components: malting, milling, mashing, brewing, cooling, fermentation, racking and finishing. Malting: The grain is added to a vat along with water and allowed to soak for about 40 hours.\\n\\npassage 3:Photo courtesy Carolina Brewing Company. The next step in the beer brewing process is called the boil. At the end of the boil we will have a finished wort (pronounced wert). To start, the liquid from the mash is put into a huge brew kettle.The one used at the Carolina Brewery holds more than 600 gallons (2,270 L).It is a steam jacketed brew kettle.n order for each batch of beer to taste the same, exactly the same amount of the same type of hops must be added at exactly the same time during each boil. In the next section, we'll see the next step in the brewing process.\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"describe the brewing process of beer\"}", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.001238613000168698, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id":