4, 3150, 1496, 11, 440, 888, 220, 17, 11, 18, 15, 15, 44651, 4512, 303, 279, 26097, 35317, 12673, 7249, 13, 10774, 39391, 8980, 513, 1608, 3349, 5038, 321, 61878, 14533, 310, 7318, 29970, 11, 6187, 310, 53053, 314, 10654, 321, 9600, 13, 17187, 8601, 310, 18526, 279, 6195, 11, 279, 4125, 8198, 264, 3478, 4492, 364, 2253, 12939, 13, 271, 55025, 198, 32, 25, 7021, 1892, 561, 2400, 1965, 369, 7021, 539, 279, 16713, 2450, 13, 198, 33, 25, 5435, 4748, 17675, 1892, 561, 1965, 5435, 39072, 2144, 13, 271, 5423, 1132, 279, 2904, 2306, 13, 198, 15666, 25], "raw_logits_by_display_label": {" A": 12.574173927307129, " B": 13.03576374053955}, "raw_logits_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 13.03576374053955, "supported": 12.574173927307129}, "readout_s": 3.6698999792861287e-05, "request_id": "sha256:acb76c52cdabb0ba9fabde85", "requested_score_position": 659, "response_sha256": "sha256:4266be8a4fa1a7e05ebe1534f02f682cb0412ac47598b6f982dade0db1fcb959", "response_text": "Here is a summary of the news article within 94 words:\n\nThe number of migrants caught crossing the US-Mexico border in the past year has reached a record high, with over 2,300 apprehensions in the Rio Grande Valley alone. Human smugglers are using large groups and stash houses to transport migrants, leading to seizures of drugs and weapons. Despite efforts to stem the flow, the issue remains a major concern for law enforcement.", "role": "calibration_tuning", "sealed_prompt_sha256": "sha256:31fdef4cae735cd46affcc0950b2a1269adc2fd6c95a2a415a303bb44dfd7fd6", "sealed_prompt_token_count": 660, "sentence_count": null, "sentence_version": null, "source_group_id": "group-e08082217a46791960a49faf", "source_sha256": "sha256:5426beb83a54d9a41bd7b5fbd4fbe52ac4d86c472beb9cdfffad8454015e1aba", "source_text": "Border Crisis: 60-year migrant records shattered\n\"Migrants were caught crossing the southern border of the U.S. more times in the past year than in any other year since at least 1960, when the government started keeping track of the data. It is the third record-setting year in a row.\" -New York Times, October 22, 2023\nAgainst the backdrop of that news, here's a snapshot of events over the past few days along the Texas-Mexico border, to show you how things are going:\nRio Grande Valley Border Patrol Agents are encountering migrants in very large groups crossing into the state. The photo above of a group of 172 migrants was taken last week. Border Patrol Officers apprehended 2,344 migrants last week in the Rio Grande Valley alone.\nBorder Patrol Agents are also encountering migrants in large numbers when raiding so-called \"stash houses\" where human smugglers hold them until they can move them deeper into the state. Pictured above are 42 migrants found in El Paso motel rooms last week.\nThis unprecedented surge of illegal crossings has human smugglers moving larger \"loads\" of migrants through Texas. The photo above is of a group of 34 migrants found inside a travel trailer last week by local authorities and Border Patrol Agents in Willacy County.\nIf you can think of a way to move through South Texas, chances are you'll find migrants using it - like the freight train shown above in Uvalde. I've written about human smugglers using Texas trains before. You can read more about that HERE.\nOf course, smugglers are moving more than migrants across the border into Texas. Rio Grande Valley agents report they seized two \"loads\" of marijuana and methamphetamines last week as well.\nIs enough being done to prevent illegal crossings of the Rio Grande into Texas?\nShare your opinions in the comments to this article.\nWant more stories like this? Be sure to \"like\" this article and follow me on theNewsbreak app!\nOther stories by Jack Beaversabout Texas Border issues include:\nTexas Border: Lawmen Killed on Both Sides of Rio Grande\nTexas Border: Tennessee Troops Deploy\nTexas Border: Cartel Gunfights & Smuggling Busts\nTexas Border: Stash House Raids Surge in October\nTexas Buses 58K Migrants to Sanctuary Cities\nMake Money Writing For Newsbreak!APPLY HERE", "source_version": {"corpus": "ragtruth", "license": "MIT", "release_revision": "c103204b9ce28d6bbad859304bf30de72b8ed8fe"}, "state_reset": true, "token_boundary_receipts": {" A": true, " B": true}, "tokenize_s": 0.0015133409997361014, "tokenizer_identity": {"bos_token_id": "248044", "eos_token_id": "248046", "metadata_model": "gpt2", "representation": "embedded_gguf"}, "window_index": null, "window_sha256": null, "window_version": null}, {"actual_last_evaluated_position": 679, "archived_pilot_reuse": false, "arm": "focused_window", "attempted": true, "byte_end": 222, "byte_start": 0, "call_id": "sha256:f12fa59807b13738469b2101", "disposition": "complete", "eligible": true, "error": null, "generated_tokens": 0, "gold_label": null, "group_id": "group-e08082217a46791960a49faf", "kv_state_id": "reset-epoch-1834", "label_to_option_id": {" A": "supported", " B": "contains_unsupported"}, "log_odds_contains_unsupported": -0.12855243682861314, "marked_response": "<<CARNOT_FOCUS_START:0:222>>Here is a summary of the news article within 94 words:\n\nThe number of migrants caught crossing the US-Mexico border in the past year has reached a record high, with over 2,300 apprehensions in the Rio Grande Valley alone. <<CARNOT_FOCUS_END>>Human smugglers are using large groups and stash houses to transport migrants, leading to seizures of drugs and weapons. Despite efforts to stem the flow, the issue remains a major concern for law enforcement.", "model_sha256": "sha256:7e78da5d7e3ae28d178121f58646953305f3e5bd3cb46f4a75584e8b6c6fe169", "native_option_token_ids": {" A": 357, " B": 417}, "normalization": "two_option_softmax_float64", "option_order": ["supported", "contains_unsupported"], "order_remapping": {" A": "supported", " B": "contains_unsupported"}, "prefill_s": 0.8297529720002785, "probabilities_by_option_id": {"contains_unsupported": 0.46790607658103, "supported": 0.53209392341897}, "prompt_sha256": "